# q09 — imported `extend`s whose methods call the library's own generic
# functions, under three kinds of interface bound.
#
# Probes: `Ranked[T: Ordered]`, `Tidied[T: Hashable]` and
# `Roster[T: Named]` — a built-in bound, another built-in bound, and one
# of the library's own interfaces as a bound. `[Int]` is both `Ordered`
# and `Hashable`, so one list answers to two of these extends at once,
# from three `extend [T] ...` lines in one file. The defaults call
# `util.top` and `util.dedup` across a module boundary the consumer never
# names, and `[T: Named]` asks whether a user interface works as a bound
# inside an `extend` target.
#
# `Player` is given an explicit `def name -> Str` rather than relying on
# its field: I could not tell from the README whether a bare field
# satisfies a required method for structural conformance, and did not
# want that question to decide this program.
#
# `best` returns a `T?`; the two `Some(...)` lines below assume the
# rendering `examples/generics.lume` shows (`Some(3)`, `Some(ada)`).
#
# Meant to RUN.
#
# NOTE (milestone 32): this header was written when how a value prints
# was undocumented. Lume now shows a value inside another in the form you
# would write it, so text inside a list, a map, an optional or a struct
# carries its quotes. The lines below are updated to match the rule.
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# Some(9)
# [3, 3, 7, 7, 9]
# [7, 3, 9]
# 3
# Some("pear")
# ["date", "fig", "pear"]
# Ada & Bo
# 2

import seqx.rank

struct Player:
  handle: Str
  score: Int

  def name -> Str = handle

def main:
  scores = [7, 3, 9, 3, 7]
  puts scores.best
  puts scores.in_order
  puts scores.once
  puts scores.once_count

  words = {"pear", "fig", "date"}
  puts words.best
  puts words.in_order

  squad = [Player("Ada", 4), Player("Bo", 6)]
  puts squad.names
  puts squad.folks.len
